---
import LessonLayout from "../layouts/LessonLayout.astro";
import Callout from "../components/Callout.astro";
import QuestionAnswer from "../components/QuestionAnswer.astro";
---

<LessonLayout
  title="How perspective works"
  description="Perspective is how near and far objects relate in your photograph. Learn how your camera position changes their apparent size, spacing and overlap."
>
  <h2>Start with where you stand</h2>
  <p>
    Picture a rock beside a lake, with a mountain in the distance. Stand close
    to the rock and it can fill much of your frame. Step back and the mountain
    becomes more prominent compared with the rock.
  </p>
  <p>
    Neither object has changed size. You've changed your viewpoint. That change
    affects how the objects look in relation to each other, which is what we
    mean by perspective.
  </p>

  <h2>Why nearby objects look larger</h2>
  <p>
    Hold one hand near your face and the other at arm's length. Your closer hand
    looks larger, even though both hands are about the same size. The same idea
    applies to objects in a photograph.
  </p>
  <p>
    Distance matters most when something is close to the camera. If you move
    from four metres away from a rock to two metres away, you halve that
    distance. The same two-metre move barely changes your distance to a mountain
    a kilometre away. That's why the rock grows much more than the mountain in
    your frame.
  </p>
  <p>
    Use this when you want a stronger foreground. Move closer, then check that
    the rock, flowers or other nearby details don't hide something you want to
    include. For a quieter foreground, try stepping back.
  </p>

  <h2>What changing focal length does</h2>
  <p>
    Focal length controls your field of view: how much of the scene your camera
    includes. From the same position, a shorter focal length shows a wider view.
    A longer focal length shows a narrower view.
  </p>
  <p>
    Zoom in without moving and both the rock and mountain grow in the frame.
    Their size relationship stays the same. You may crop out the rock entirely,
    but cropping it out isn't a change in perspective.
  </p>
  <Callout title="Choose your position, then your framing">
    <p>
      Move the camera to change how objects relate. Once you like that
      relationship, choose a focal length that includes the part of the scene
      you want.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Why a longer lens can seem to bring the background closer</h2>
  <p>
    Suppose you use a longer focal length, then step back to keep the rock about
    the same size in your photograph. The mountain now looks larger compared
    with the rock. The scene may seem flatter, with less space between its
    layers.
  </p>
  <p>
    Stepping back caused that perspective change. The longer focal length helped
    you keep similar framing. This combination is often called compression, but
    the lens hasn't moved the mountain closer.
  </p>

  <h2>Move sideways or change height</h2>
  <p>
    Distance isn't your only choice. If a tree overlaps a mountain summit, take
    a small step sideways. The nearby tree shifts across your view more than the
    distant mountain, which can separate them.
  </p>
  <p>
    Raising the camera may reveal a path hidden behind a rock. Lowering it may
    hide that path or place the rock against a different part of the background.
    Check the overlap instead of assuming that a lower viewpoint always gives
    more depth.
  </p>
  <p>
    Keep height and tilt separate. In this lab's level-camera model, raising the
    camera changes foreground placement while the horizon stays centred. Tilt
    downward to move the horizon higher in the frame and include more ground.
  </p>

  <h2>Try a simple comparison</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Open the simulator and save its starting view as comparison A.</li>
    <li>
      Keep fixed camera position selected. Increase focal length and watch the
      rock-to-mountain height ratio stay the same.
    </li>
    <li>
      Reset the camera. Turn on maintain subject size, then increase focal
      length again. Watch the camera move back and the ratio change.
    </li>
    <li>
      Save comparison B. Compare the mountain's size while the rock stays
      similarly framed.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Try the same exercise outdoors with a nearby object and a distant feature.
    Keep your camera pointed in the same direction when comparing focal lengths.
    Make one change at a time so you can see what caused the difference.
  </p>

  <QuestionAnswer question="Does a wide-angle lens automatically exaggerate perspective?">
    <p>
      No. A close viewpoint creates a strong difference between near and far
      objects. A wide-angle lens lets you include more of the scene from that
      position. Changing to a wider lens without moving doesn't create a new
      size relationship.
    </p>
  </QuestionAnswer>
  <QuestionAnswer question="Is there one correct perspective for a landscape?">
    <p>
      No. A large foreground can lead attention into a scene, while a more
      balanced size relationship can give the background greater presence.
      Choose the viewpoint that helps show what caught your attention.
    </p>
  </QuestionAnswer>
  <p>
    <a href="/perspective-simulator/">Try the perspective simulator</a>
    {" or "}
    <a href="/camera-distance-perspective/">
      explore camera distance and perspective
    </a>
    .
  </p>
</LessonLayout>
